# Why I think this is scientifically useful

The strongest reason is **not** that it reproduces quantum tricks.

It lets us ask a much more useful question:

> **Which phenomena commonly associated with quantum mechanics actually require complex amplitudes and probabilities, and which survive in a finite Boolean-linear/modal theory?**

That is a genuine foundations question.

For example, our current work already separates several things that are often mentally bundled together:

```math
\text{superposition} \not\Rightarrow \text{probability}
```

```math
\text{interference} \not\Rightarrow \text{complex amplitudes}
```

```math
\text{nonseparability} \not\Rightarrow \text{universal teleportation}
```

and, particularly interestingly in the chain-ring model,

```math
\text{strong contextuality} \not\Rightarrow \text{universal teleportation resource}.
```

That last one is much more than a pedagogical curiosity. We already found strongly contextual singular resources that fail universal teleportation because the relevant resource invariant is module rank/unit determinant.

So the model becomes a **controlled laboratory for pulling quantum concepts apart**.

That strikes me as one of the strongest uses of all the "non-novel" phenomena.

---

# Where LMs could make this substantially more distinctive

This is where I think there is unexplored potential.

Rather than simply having a finite-field quantum model, we have a prior logical language.

Imagine an experiment written symbolically as an LM:

```math
\mathcal L(x,y,\ldots)
```

where its entries may themselves be formulas.

It can then be:

```math
\text{symbolic LM} \rightarrow \text{Boolean valuation} \rightarrow \text{CM coefficient representation} \rightarrow \text{phase/state circuit} \rightarrow \text{LM/effect readout}.
```

That creates a **logic-to-experiment pipeline**.

There are several directions this could support.

One is **symbolic observables**. Instead of an observable merely being an anonymous row vector, an outcome could carry an actual formula such as

```math
x\land\neg y
```

or a relational condition between components.

Another is **Boolean oracles**. The nonlinear Boolean logic can be retained as oracle/control logic without contaminating the XOR-linear state space. The earlier phase investigation already arrived at essentially this division:

- linear XOR/rotation/tensor operations for state evolution;
- arbitrary Boolean connectives for predicates/oracles/control;
- LM/support operations for measurement/readout.

That separation deserves to be formalized.

A third possibility is **symbolic compilation of experiments**. Paper B's folding machinery may allow an LM-level description of an experiment to be simplified before expansion into large block matrices.

That would connect the theoretical quantum-like work back to an actual computational use of the CM/LM machinery.

