# I would make one feature especially prominent: the "quantum-feature dependency map"

This might actually become one of the most useful outputs.

Instead of simply cataloguing phenomena, ask what algebraic ingredients each one requires.

For example:

```math
\begin{array}{c|ccccc} & \oplus & \text{phase} & \otimes & \text{zero divisors} & \text{probability}\\ \hline \text{superposition-like states} & \checkmark&&&&\\ \text{interference} & \checkmark&\checkmark&&&\\ \text{nonseparability} & \checkmark&&\checkmark&&\\ \text{Bell support contradiction} & \checkmark&&\checkmark&&\\ \text{Hardy valuation hierarchy} & \checkmark&&\checkmark&\checkmark&\\ \text{teleportation} & \checkmark&?&\checkmark&&\\ \text{Born statistics} &&&&&\checkmark \end{array}
```

Obviously we'd make the actual table mathematically precise.

Then we can systematically remove ingredients:

- remove the `C_4` phase;
- replace `A` with `\mathbb F_2`;
- remove zero divisors;
- enlarge the local operation group;
- switch from `A`-tensor to literal `\mathbb F_2`-tensor;
- restrict measurement families.

And observe **exactly which phenomena disappear**.

That turns the work from a catalogue into something closer to a scientific decomposition of quantum-like structure.

